{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 frames the 'if and only if' of panel superiority as turning on conditional independence of panel-member errors given the checklist's features. But this criterion is not identifiable within a single evaluation. The target asks the evaluation to distinguish 'genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer,' yet its proposed diagnostic—measuring whether errors cluster on non-checklisted cases—cannot separate two indistinguishable outcomes: (a) errors are conditionally independent given checklist features but the checklist is incomplete, producing clustered failures via shared feature omission, and (b) errors are correlated through shared training on the checklist, producing the same clustered failures. Both yield identical within-panel agreement patterns on incorrect items. The biconditional claim ('if and only if') thus overstates what a single evaluation can establish: it can detect error clustering, but cannot adjudicate whether the clustering stems from feature-space incompleteness (a coverage problem) or from training-induced correlation (an independence problem), which the target itself identifies as a distinct mechanism. The covering principle the target seeks—conditional independence—is not observable from the data a single evaluation provides.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Two panels evaluated on the same 100 items yield identical results: 15 incorrect items, all failed by a majority of members, with high within-panel agreement on those failures. In Panel A, the failures are due to shared checklist omission (all members lack the discriminative feature). In Panel B, the failures are due to training-induced correlation (members were coached on the same heuristics). The within-panel agreement diagnostic cannot distinguish these, yet they have different implications for whether aggregation can help.","identical_observable":"Both panels: same accuracy, same error clustering pattern, same within-panel agreement on incorrect items"}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 concludes the claim is 'false'—asserting expert superiority—by attributing expert advantage to tacit feature recognition that checklists exclude. But this commits a domain-composition fallacy: it assumes tacit features are present in the task domain without establishing their prevalence, then treats their existence as sufficient to reverse the average. The target's own countercondition ('The task domain has no tacit discriminative features') concedes that for domains where all discriminative features are articulable, the argument collapses—but the target never addresses how often this countercondition obtains, instead presupposing tacit features are generally present. For tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, many domains (e.g., rule-based classification, structured scoring) have fully articulable discriminative features, making the checklist sufficient and expert intuition's 'tacit' advantage nonexistent. The argument thus fails to establish the claim is false 'on average' across the problem's domain space; it only argues for a subset where tacit features happen to dominate.","counterexample":[{"domain":"Loan application eligibility screening with fixed criteria (income, debt-to-income ratio, credit score thresholds)","outcome":"All discriminative features are fully articulable and captured in the checklist; expert intuition provides no tacit-feature advantage, and panel aggregation of checklist-compliant judgments outperforms a single expert","point":"In this objectively-verifiable-outcome domain, SRC_002's premise (tacit features exist and checklists exclude them) is false, so its conclusion that the expert wins does not follow"}]}]}